**New evidence**
- Shehbaz Sharif’s April 2022 outreach to Narendra Modi is the cleanest direct ruler-level peace signal in the file: he explicitly asked for meaningful dialogue, peaceful/cooperative ties, and regional peace/security.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Seek meaningful talks with India, peaceful ties: Shehbaz Sharif to PM Modi","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-04-17","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/seek-meaningful-talks-with-india-peaceful-ties-shehbaz-to-modi-7872783/","claim":"Shehbaz Sharif wrote to Narendra Modi seeking meaningful dialogue, peaceful and cooperative ties, and regional peace and security; he also framed Kashmir resolution as part of that settlement.","locator":"lines 95-97, 123-125","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent mainstream reporting quotes the letter and Sharif's own tweet from the same exchange.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period after Sharif took office in April 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Sharif statement; strong personal attribution.","contrary_evidence":["This is diplomatic rhetoric and does not by itself prove durable policy change or concessions."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Pakistan’s foreign ministry said, a week after Sharif took office, that cross-border attacks from Afghanistan had risen and that Pakistan wanted Afghanistan to secure the border and act against militants.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PRESS RELEASE","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-04-17","url":"https://mofa.gov.pk/press-release-496","claim":"Pakistan said cross-border attacks from Afghanistan had risen, asked Kabul to secure the border, condemned militant use of Afghan soil, and reaffirmed respect for Afghan sovereignty.","locator":"lines 119-122","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated official government statement with concrete border-security claims and a de-escalatory closing line.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and immediately after Sharif took office.","ruler_attribution":"Government attribution is strong, but this is the foreign ministry's line rather than a personal PM directive.","contrary_evidence":["Afghan authorities denied the accusations, so the statement is one-sided and not a neutral finding."],"lenses":["2B.5","2B.6","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- Reuters’ April 2022 reporting shows the border situation with Afghanistan was already active and ambiguous: Pakistan said its forces were being targeted, while the report says the flare-up followed purported Pakistani air strikes inside Afghan airspace.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan says cross-border attacks from Afghanistan have increased","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-04-18","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/04/18/us-afghanistan-pakistan","claim":"Pakistan said its forces were being targeted from Afghanistan and the incident followed purported Pakistani air strikes inside Afghan airspace; UNAMA voiced concern about reported civilian casualties.","locator":"lines 660-680","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is a strong independent source; the piece also records UNAMA concern, which adds corroborating monitoring context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period.","ruler_attribution":"This is government border-policy context under Sharif; the report does not prove Sharif personally ordered the alleged strikes.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not independently verify the alleged Pakistani air strikes, and Pakistan denied carrying them out."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- In May 2022, Taliban-mediated talks produced a temporary ceasefire with a local Pakistani Taliban group, showing active peace-channel use even though the attribution to the central government is not perfectly clear.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Afghanistan's Taliban mediate ceasefire between Pakistan, local militants","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/05/19/us-pakistan-afghanistan-ceasefire","claim":"The Taliban in Afghanistan mediated a temporary ceasefire between Pakistan and a local Pakistani Taliban group after talks in Kabul.","locator":"lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a live diplomatic/security process with direct attribution from Taliban and TTP statements.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Pakistan side at the government/security level, but Reuters says it was not clear who represented Pakistan in the talks.","contrary_evidence":["The ceasefire was temporary, and the reporting does not identify a clear PM-level negotiator."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.5","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- By November 2022, the Pakistan-Afghanistan frontier was still producing armed clashes severe enough to shut a major crossing.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan-Afghan border crossing closed after clashes","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/15/afghanistan-pakistan","claim":"A major border crossing closed after prolonged firing between security forces from both sides, with the Taliban describing the incident as a misunderstanding.","locator":"lines 641-646","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting provides a clear contemporaneous account of the clash and closure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period.","ruler_attribution":"Border-management crisis under Sharif, but responsibility for initiating the clash was disputed.","contrary_evidence":["Both sides blamed each other and the Taliban said it was a misunderstanding, so initiation is not cleanly attributable."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- In December 2022, Pakistan publicly condemned the Chaman shootings and stressed both civilian protection and the need to prevent recurrence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Release","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-12-11","url":"https://mofa.gov.pk/press-release-547","claim":"Pakistan said six Pakistani nationals were killed and 17 wounded after Afghan border forces fired on civilians in Chaman, and urged strict action to avoid recurrence.","locator":"search-result snippet / press release summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official statement with specific casualty and de-escalation language, but the underlying incident account is contested by the Afghan side.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period.","ruler_attribution":"Government response under Sharif; not a personal order or battlefield decision by the PM.","contrary_evidence":["Afghan authorities likely had a different account of the incident, so the casualty and responsibility framing is contested."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- A follow-up foreign-ministry statement on the Chaman incident stressed both sides’ responsibility to protect civilians and keep border peace.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Afghan Chargé d’Affaires called to the Foreign Ministry over Chaman Incident","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://mofa.gov.pk/press-releases/afghan-charge-daffaires-called-to-the-foreign-ministry-over-chaman-incident","claim":"Pakistan condemned cross-border shelling in Chaman-Spin Boldak, said civilian protection was a shared responsibility, and said peace along the border was intrinsic to fraternal relations.","locator":"lines 110-112","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated official statement with direct de-escalatory language and explicit border-peace framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period.","ruler_attribution":"Official government response under Sharif, though still not a direct PM operational order.","contrary_evidence":["This statement does not settle who initiated the incident or whether later steps reduced the underlying border risk."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 25-40 distinct documents across Reuters mirrors, Euronews, Indian Express, Dawn, MOFA, CTBTO-adjacent material, and a few broader Pakistan/region pages.
- I kept only items that either showed Sharif’s own peace messaging, showed foreign-policy conduct under his government, or documented concrete border incidents and ceasefire efforts.
- I rejected out-of-period items from before April 11, 2022 as direct Sharif evidence, including the March Afghanistan neighbor-fm meeting and the March joint statement, though they were useful as inherited-baseline context.
- I rejected repeated syndications of the same Reuters stories when they added no new factual content.
- I rejected broad profile pages and generic regional explainers that did not improve 2B attribution or conduct.
- I also kept the earlier March 2022 India missile incident only as reused crisis context from the prior chapter, not as new 2B evidence.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no clean PM-level public record of Sharif personally ordering a major cross-border military action or personally directing the Afghanistan channel.
- The April 2022 Afghanistan-airstrike allegations remain attribution-weak: the reporting suggests Pakistani action may have occurred, but the government denial means the personal chain of command is unresolved.
- The ceasefire with the local Pakistani Taliban was real, but the public record does not identify who represented Pakistan, so the ruler-specific peace credit is partial.
- More search would help only if it surfaces PMO, cabinet, NSC, or archival diplomatic records that tie a specific 2022 de-escalation or escalation decision directly to Sharif.
